index c9a7c533..5cbc913f 100644
--- a/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
+++ b/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
@@ -48,13 +48,11 @@ As of f6c46b59. The repository keeps no decision records and no architecture doc
 - AD-3 (inherited) All state lives in one relational database reached through the ORM, on SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB alike; schema changes are numbered migrations per app; code must not rely on engine-specific behaviour, and query code is written to avoid known slow plans (sort by `created`, not `id`). Why: the README names these database families as the supported building blocks, and the same code and tests must run on all of them (stated: `README.md` "PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB"). Evidence: `hc/settings.py` `DATABASES`; `.github/workflows/tests.yml` `matrix`.
 - AD-4 (inherited) Alert delivery is a pull model with no broker: the web process (or the alert process itself) writes a status change row, and the separate `sendalerts` process polls for unprocessed rows and delivers them; there is no scheduler and no retry table. Why: the web process only records the change, and the separate alert process picks it up and processes it, using the same rows for downtime statistics (stated: `hc/api/models.py` "picks it up and processes it"). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `create_flip`; `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `process_one_flip`.
 - AD-5 (inherited) Work is claimed with optimistic conditional UPDATEs that must change exactly one row (a status change is claimed by setting its processed time where it is still empty; a check goes down by an update guarded on its old status; reports claim by their next date), so several processes can run without locks. Why: several worker processes may share one database, and a lost race is simply skipped (stated: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` "another sendalerts process got there first"). Evidence: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `handle_going_down`; `hc/api/management/commands/sendreports.py` `handle_one_report`.
-- AD-6 (inherited) Delivery is at most once per status change and integration: the status change is marked processed before any integration is contacted, a failed attempt is recorded and never re-queued, and an escaped exception or crash abandons the remaining integrations of that status change; retries exist only inside one attempt. Why: marking the work done first prevents duplicate alerts from concurrent workers or restarts, at the price of no recovery after a crash (inferred). Evidence: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `num_updated`; `hc/api/transports.py` `request`.
 - AD-7 (inherited) `sendalerts` is one long-running management command: a poll loop with a 2 second idle sleep, a thread pool of `--num-workers` (default 1), signal handlers that let running deliveries finish, stale DB connections closed per thread, and the DB application name set to the command; operators must keep it running, and the Docker image stops the container when any child exits. Why: alerts are a continuous duty of an instance, so operators must supervise the process and containers must fail loudly when it dies (stated: `README.md` "Running in Production"). Evidence: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `handle`; `docker/run.sh` `wait -n`; `CHANGELOG.md` "v5.0 - 2026-10-20".
 - AD-8 (inherited) One transport class per integration kind, registered in the `TRANSPORTS` table in `hc/api/models.py` and imported on first use; a transport implements `notify(flip, notification)` and, when it can ignore a status, `is_noop(status)`; HTTP kinds extend `HttpTransport` and Slack-compatible kinds extend `Slackalike`. Why: a uniform contract lets the alert process treat every kind alike and keeps each kind's code in its own app (stated: `CONTRIBUTING.md` "Developing a New Integration"). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `TRANSPORTS`; `hc/api/transports.py` `HttpTransport`.
 - AD-9 (inherited) Delivery errors are the typed exception `TransportError(message, permanent)`: the message is user-facing text that is stored and shown verbatim; `permanent` disables the integration and ends in-attempt retries; any other exception is treated as a bug and propagates out of the attempt. Why: it separates expected remote failures, which users must see, from bugs, which go to the log, and lets a transport say "stop trying" (inferred). Evidence: `hc/api/transports.py` `TransportError`; `hc/api/models.py` `transports.TransportError`.
 - AD-10 (inherited) One delivery attempt is one `Notification` row, inserted before sending with the error text "Sending" and finalized afterwards; later callbacks address the same row through its random public `code`; the `Channel` columns `last_notify`, `last_notify_duration` and `last_error` are a denormalized summary of the latest attempt, overwritten each time. Why: the row must exist before sending so provider callbacks and bounces can name it and so in-progress attempts are visible (inferred). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `n.error = "Sending"`; `hc/api/views.py` `notification_status`.
 - AD-11 (inherited) `Channel.notify` is the single entry point for one delivery and returns its outcome as text ("" for success, "no-op", or the error); the alert process, the Test! view, the group transport and the account-deletion command all call it and read that text. Why: one choke point keeps record keeping, integration state and quota use consistent across the four callers (inferred). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `notify`; `hc/integrations/group/transport.py` `notify`.
-- AD-12 (inherited) Retrying is a per-transport concern inside one attempt: `HttpTransport.request` makes up to 3 immediate tries, Signal 2, email sending 3 with a 1 second pause; transports opt out through `permanent` errors, and the webhook transport opts out for test notifications; there is no shared policy object, no back-off, no stored attempt counter and no setting. Why: transports differ in protocol, so each handles transient errors itself, and for webhooks the policy is promised to users (stated: `hc/integrations/webhook/templates/webhook_form.html` "retries failed webhook calls up to 2 additional times"). Evidence: `hc/api/transports.py` `tries_left`; `hc/integrations/signal/transport.py` `tries_left`; `hc/lib/emails.py` `MAX_TRIES`.
 - AD-13 (inherited) Quotas and rate limits are enforced inside each transport at send time, through account counters (`authorize_sms`, `authorize_call` on the profile) and `TokenBucket` rows; a refused attempt is an ordinary failed attempt with a short text, so each call to a transport spends quota. Why: SMS and calls cost money per use, so the check sits next to the provider call (inferred). Evidence: `hc/integrations/sms/transport.py` `authorize_sms`; `hc/api/models.py` `authorize_telegram`.
 - AD-14 (inherited) A check's event log is assembled per request from the ping, notification and status-change tables, merged and sorted in Python, and rendered by shared row templates for the Events page, its live fragment and the details page; there is no unified event table and no stored per-event rendering. Why: retention limits the events per check, so three small queries per request are cheap and no duplicate event table is needed (inferred). Evidence: `hc/front/views.py` `_get_events`; `templates/front/log_row.html` `event.check_status`.
 - AD-15 (inherited) Retention of notifications and status changes is derived from ping retention (`Profile.ping_log_limit`, default 100) and runs inline on every 100th ping plus an optional batch command; there is no separate retention setting. Why: one knob keeps the log consistent, and status changes are kept for about three months because downtime statistics need them (stated: `hc/api/models.py` "We need ~3 months of flips for calculating"). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `prune`; `hc/api/management/commands/prunepingsslow.py` `Command`.
@@ -70,6 +68,13 @@ As of f6c46b59. The repository keeps no decision records and no architecture doc
 - AD-25 (inherited) Tests run with Django's runner, one `tests/` package per app; CI runs them on SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL and MariaDB with Python 3.12 to 3.15, plus `mypy --strict` and a coverage run; `TransactionTestCase` appears once, where a test needs real commits and an `IntegrityError`. Why: the supported-environment promises are only proven by running the suite on each of them (inferred). Evidence: `.github/workflows/tests.yml` `matrix`; `hc/api/tests/test_notify.py` `TransactionTestCase`.
 - AD-26 (inherited) Static typing is enforced: every module starts with `from __future__ import annotations` and the tree must pass `mypy --strict` with the django-stubs plugin; formatting is ruff. Why: the style section asks for ruff and simplicity, and the strict type check runs in CI on every push (stated: `CONTRIBUTING.md` "Code Style"). Evidence: `.github/workflows/mypy.yml` `mypy --strict`; `mypy.ini` `django-stubs`.
 - AD-27 (inherited) User-visible changes are written into `CHANGELOG.md` under the top "## v..." heading, and the application's displayed version is read from the first such heading at start-up. Why: the changelog is the release record and the running version is derived from it (stated: `CHANGELOG.md` "All notable changes"). Evidence: `hc/settings.py` `VERSION`; `CHANGELOG.md` "Unreleased".
+- AD-28 (accepted) A failed delivery is no longer final. Delivery stays claim-first as in the superseded AD-6 (the status change is marked processed before any integration is contacted, and a crash or escaped exception still abandons that status change's remaining integrations), but a non-permanent failed attempt is re-queued on its own `Notification` row: the `sendalerts` loop polls for due retries after its flip step, claims one with a conditional UPDATE that clears the due time before delivering (as AD-5), and delivers through `Channel.notify` (AD-11) on the existing worker pool; a crash after the claim loses that retry instead of duplicating it. Why: S1 needs a stored due time and something to wake up for it; the pull loop, its pool, its signal handling and its claim rule already exist, so a retry is one more poll and operators run nothing new. Alternatives: a broker with delayed tasks (Celery, RQ) or OS timers (new infrastructure for self-hosters, against AD-4); a separate retry table (a second record to keep in step with the log, pruning and cascades); sleeping 25 minutes in a worker thread (blocks the `--num-workers` pool, lost on restart); leasing a retry by pushing its due time forward (survives crashes but can send duplicate alerts, which AD-6 avoided). Basis: S1
+- AD-29 (accepted) Retry policy: the per-transport in-attempt tries stay as they are (HTTP kinds 3 tries, Signal 2, email 3, the webhook test opt-out) and run inside every attempt; on top, a platform-level delayed retry gives each integration delivery up to 3 retries, due 1, 5 and 25 minutes after the failure of the previous attempt, as an explicit tuple of timedeltas in `hc/api/models.py` with no setting and no change to any transport. Eligible: a `TransportError` that is not `permanent` (AD-9), returned for a saved status change. Not eligible: permanent errors; successes and no-ops; test notifications and account-deletion notices (unsaved dummy status changes, answered to a user at once); the group integration's own attempt (its members each retry on their own, so members that already succeeded are not sent again). Why: S1 fixes the count and the delays; placing the policy in `Channel.notify` gives every kind the same behaviour without per-kind code (AD-8) and keeps the superseded AD-12's per-transport handling of transient errors intact; a constant matches the existing constants (30 second timeout, 3 tries) and avoids a documented setting nobody asked for (AD-18). Alternatives: per-transport retries (one edit per kind and no shared delays); a formula such as 5**(n-1) (less explicit, harder to test); a settings entry for the delays; a non-retryable flag on `TransportError` to exempt quota refusals (changes AD-9). Basis: S1
+- AD-30 (accepted) Retry state is three columns on `Notification`, added by one additive migration `0124` in `hc/api/migrations` (no backfill, no data migration): `flip` (nullable foreign key to `Flip`, CASCADE: the status change being delivered; null for test notifications, deletion notices and rows from before the migration), `attempt` (small integer, default 1: 1 for the first delivery, 2 to 4 for retries) and `retry_at` (nullable datetime: when the next retry is due, null when none is pending), with a conditional index on `retry_at` like the two indexes the alert process already polls (`api_flip_not_processed`, `api_check_aa_not_down`). `Channel.notify` gets an optional `attempt` argument, sets `flip` only when the passed status change is saved (most integration tests pass an unsaved one, so they leave `flip` empty and schedule nothing, and the few that save one succeed) and writes `retry_at` in the same UPDATE that records the error. Retention and deletion follow the row (AD-15): pruning, clearing events and deleting the check, integration or status change also drop pending retries. Late provider callbacks and bounces save only the `error` column (`save(update_fields=["error"])`), so they never overwrite `retry_at`. Environments: the `sendalerts` process and the web process both run `Channel.notify`; the database is SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB through the same migration (AD-3); all new names live in the `hc/api` app (`Notification.flip`, `Notification.attempt`, `Notification.retry_at`, a delays constant in `hc/api/models.py`, a retry step and worker function in `sendalerts.py`) and collide with nothing there. Why: the attempt row already exists before sending and outlives the attempt (AD-10), so the due time gives the poller one indexed query and ties the retry's lifetime to the log row it explains. Alternatives: a new table (see AD-28); `retry_at` on `Flip` (one status change fans out to many integrations, each with its own chain); rebuilding the status change from check and status at retry time (ambiguous when several changes queue up); keeping the attempt count only in the error text (not queryable); `SET_NULL` on the flip (leaves pending retries with no status change to deliver). Basis: S1
+- AD-31 (accepted) Each retry is its own `Notification` row, so the existing Events log lists every attempt with its error and the display path stays as it is: `_get_events` and its "Downtime alert" filter, `event_summary.html` with its "Error:" line and the live poll's created-after cursor are reused unchanged; no new page, endpoint, API field, template or setting, and access follows AD-16 exactly as for the first attempt. Why: S1 asks to show attempts where past notifications are listed today; a row per attempt carries its own error, its own start time and its own provider callback address, and the live feed finds it because it was created later. Alternatives: update one row in place (hides later attempts from the live feed and keeps one 200-character error); one row per delivery with an attempt history column; a new attempts panel or Management API listing; an attempt-number label in `event_summary.html` (not asked for). Basis: S1
 
 ## Superseded
-None. The repository keeps no decision records, so no record is superseded (linked decision records: 0).
+- AD-6 (superseded by AD-28) Delivery is at most once per status change and integration: the status change is marked processed before any integration is contacted, a failed attempt is recorded and never re-queued, and an escaped exception or crash abandons the remaining integrations of that status change; retries exist only inside one attempt. Why: marking the work done first prevents duplicate alerts from concurrent workers or restarts, at the price of no recovery after a crash (inferred). Evidence: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `num_updated`; `hc/api/transports.py` `request`. Basis: S1 (asks that a failed delivery be retried after 1, 5 and 25 minutes, so a failed attempt is no longer final)
+- AD-12 (superseded by AD-29) Retrying is a per-transport concern inside one attempt: `HttpTransport.request` makes up to 3 immediate tries, Signal 2, email sending 3 with a 1 second pause; transports opt out through `permanent` errors, and the webhook transport opts out for test notifications; there is no shared policy object, no back-off, no stored attempt counter and no setting. Why: transports differ in protocol, so each handles transient errors itself, and for webhooks the policy is promised to users (stated: `hc/integrations/webhook/templates/webhook_form.html` "retries failed webhook calls up to 2 additional times"). Evidence: `hc/api/transports.py` `tries_left`; `hc/integrations/signal/transport.py` `tries_left`; `hc/lib/emails.py` `MAX_TRIES`. Basis: S1 (asks for exponential back-off with a stored count; the per-transport in-attempt tries it describes stand and are restated in AD-29)
+
+The repository keeps no decision records (linked decision records: 0); the two entries above are the only ones superseded.
